    "title": "Konnor Griffin contract details: Pirates phenom finalizing largest deal in franchise history before playing single MLB game | Sporting News",
    "text": "The Pirates are wasting no time locking down their future. On Thursday, the franchise announced that it was promoting SS Konnor Griffin -- baseball's No. 1 prospect -- to the big-league roster. With the announcement, Griffin becomes the first teenager to play an MLB game since Junior Caminero was called up in 2023.The hits kept on coming for Pittsburgh on Thursday, with ESPN's Buster Olney reporting that the Bucs were nearing a record contract with Griffin. The deal has yet to be finalized, per Olney. However, its terms would see Griffin, still just 19 years old, take home more money than any Pirate has in franchise history. Here's what you need to know.SN's MLB HQ: Live MLB scores | Updated MLB standings | Full MLB scheduleKonnor Griffin contract detailsYears: NineValue: $140 millionThe Pirates' proposed deal will see Griffin earn $140 million over nine years, good for an average annual value (AAV) of $15.55 million. Olney reported that Pittsburgh's initial proposals were \"closer in the range\" to the eight-year, $111 million deal Corbin Carroll inked with the Diamondbacks in 2023.Griffin and his negotiating team, by comparison, sought a contract closer to the eight-year, $130 million contract Roman Anthony landed with the Red Sox last August.MORE: How No. 1 prospect Konnor Griffin could fit into Pirates' 2026 lineupIt seems Griffin's side won out. His new deal easily takes the cake as the franchise's largest-ever contract handed out, surpassing the eight-year, $106.5 million deal the Pirates agreed to with Bryan Reynolds in 2023.There's a case to be made that Griffin is missing out on money, too. If Griffin is able to live up to his potential, he likely would have commanded a contract worth double or triple the amount he is due to sign for.Why did Pirates extend Konnor Griffin before his first MLB game?Griffin is the latest in a string of top prospects to command big money quite early in their big-league careers. On March 31, the Mariners agreed terms to an eight-year, $95 million extension. The contract also features a full no-trade clause and escalators that, if hit, would bring the contract's value north of $140 million.As previously mentioned, Carroll and Anthony fielded similar deals in Arizona and Boston, respectively. The Pirates' extension follow the same logic as its predecessors; if Griffin succeeds, they'll save lots of money, securing their future while not sacrificing their rigid payroll structure.The Bucs could wield Griffin in any way they so desire, using the rest of their payroll to build around him and Paul Skenes or dangling him as a trade piece with an inexpensive contract. Either way, Pittsburgh's front office will be delighted with the transaction. Even if Griffin doesn't reach his impressive potential, the contract should be a win for Griffin's employers. $15.55 million a year isn't all that much for a league-average shortstop, after all -- players to earn more money than that in recent years include Javier Baez, Dansby Swanson, Willy Adames and Ha-seong Kim (albeit on a short-term deal).Largest contracts in Pirates historyAssuming Griffin and the Pirates do agree to the terms laid out in Olney's report, he'll surge to the top of the payment charts in the Steel City. Here's how his contract compares to other Pittsburgh luminaries past and present.PlayerLengthValueKonnor Griffin9$140 millionBryan Reynolds8$111 millionMitch Keller5$77 millionKe'Bryan Hayes7$70 millionJason Kendall6$60 millionAndrew McCutchen6$51.5 millionBrian Giles6$45 millionFrancisco Liriano3$39 millionGregory Polanco5$35 millionFrancisco Cervelli3$31 millionStarling Marte3$31 millionHow old is Konnor Griffin?Griffin is 19 years old. He'll turn 20 on April 24. When he makes his debut on April 2, Griffin will become the first teenage to take part in a Major League game since Junior Caminero in 2023.",
